  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 220 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nAs troughing and surface low moves off to the northeast, another \nridge will build into the region Thursday through Friday. Even \nwith the cold front moving through, Thursday highs will still \nremain slightly above seasonal norms for this time of year in \nthe 50s. This is about 8 to 12 degrees above normal. Friday will\nbe more of the same, albeit even warmer with highs in the mid \nto upper 50s across the eastern Panhandle, climbing to the mid \n60s further east in potions of north central to central \nNebraska. There even is some low end chances 10 - 30 percent \nchance for southwest Nebraska cresting above 65 degrees. By \nFriday evening another system, guidance suggesting a weaker open\nwave solution, will trek across the Rockies and move into the \nNorthern Plains. \n\nA cold front is expected to develop and push across the eastern \nDakotas/Nebraska Friday evening into Saturday morning that could \nbring some precipitation chances across the eastern portions of the \nCWA. Daytime highs over the weekend will return to near normals, to \neven a little below normal with highs in the 40s across the region. \nWith overnight lows Friday night into Saturday being in the mid to \nupper 30s, precipitation type would likely be rain, maybe some mixed \nrain/snow chances. This however should be noted that total \naccumulations remain low at this time. LREF guidance only has a 20 \nto 40 percent chance of total rainfall being greater than an a tenth \nof an inch. Overall, guidance has trended away from any large storms \ntaking place over the weekend, with recent models keeping the any \nsignificant precipitation much further north into North Dakota. This \nshould still be watched closely, as recent past guidance had quite a \nbit of inconsistencies with this system. \n\nHeading into early next week, upper flow rinses and repeats like a \nroller coaster, with another ridging quickly building in and an \nupper low making landfall in the Pacific and moving eastward towards \nour area later in the week. Daytime highs however, will not climb \nback above seasonal norms, remaining in the upper 30s to low 40s \nacross the area Monday.",
